{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1280,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How did we get from this to this ?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In some ways the answer just boils down to \n“climate change” - we need way more renewable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy, so it makes sense that lots and lots \nof engineering and economic resources have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gone into improving and enlarging windmills. But \nwhile “climate change” can explain why windmills  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have pushed towards really good design, it \ndoesn’t explain what makes a design good."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I see three main features to explain: the size, \nthe number of blades, and the shape of the blades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Size is easiest: the bigger the area , the more \nwind you can use, and therefore the more wind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy you can capture . Plus, the higher up you \ngo, the less the wind itself is impeded by stuff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the ground, the faster it blows, and therefore \nthe more wind energy you can capture . So for a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "windmill to have access to air with a lot \nof energy , it should be giant and tall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, a paradox of windmills is that they need \nto capture energy from the wind while also letting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wind past. If you extracted 100% of the \nkinetic energy from the wind, it would stop moving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there’d be nowhere for incoming wind to go. \nSo you have to let some wind through - calculation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shows that a mathematically ideal windmill can \nonly extract 59% of the wind’s kinetic energy ."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66240,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since windmills can’t block the wind \ntoo much, they’re faced with a tradeoff:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either have fast-moving blades that cover a small \namount of area, or slow-moving blades that cover  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a large amount of area. This is because, just as \nan airplane wing produces more lift the faster the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plane is moving, a windmill blade “catches” more \nof the wind the faster it’s moving - so roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speaking, a fast-moving windmill, like modern \nones, needs correspondingly fewer, thinner blades  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in order to not slow the wind too much, while a \nslow-moving windmill can have more, wider blades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Obviously, modern windmills have gone with \nthe narrow, fast approach. So why modern  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "windmills are designed to spin more quickly than \nold windmills - I mean, if slower were better,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there would be no reason modern windmills \ncouldn’t look like giant high-tech sails!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer comes from Newton’s third law: just as \nthe wind pushes the blades sideways to turn them,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113040,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the blades push back on the wind, \ngiving the air a reverse twist,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and hence some rotational kinetic energy - \nwhich is energy the windmill doesn’t capture.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121280,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the most efficient windmill will give \nthe wind the smallest twist possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, you guessed it, the faster a windmill blade \nmoves, the less rotational energy it gives to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129520,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wind. This might seem a little counter-intuitive, \nbut a similar thing happens when a ball falls and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bounces off an angled block - if the block isn’t \nmoving, conservation of momentum and energy mean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138560,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the ball bounces to the left and the block \ngets pushed right. But if the block starts off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving to the right, it’s able to absorb more \nof the ball’s energy when it accelerates.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The faster the block moves, the more energy \nit extracts from the ball! (You can see this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the ball moves less each time). Windmill \nblades are a bit more complicated, but it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roughly the same idea - for decent efficiency, a \nwindmill blade should be moving through the air at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "least five times faster than the incoming speed of \nthe wind… though obviously different parts of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "windmill blade are moving at different speeds and \nso the shape varies along the length of the blade."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in summary: an ideal power-generating \nwindmill is big to capture a lot of wind,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tall to capture strong winds, fast-moving \nto be most efficient, and narrow-bladed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because a fast-moving windmill (needs \nto have a smaller blade area - aka,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182400,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a few, narrow blades) to \nnot slow down the wind too much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187520,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video was created in partnership \nwith Bill Gates, inspired by his new book  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“How to Avoid a Climate Disaster.” You can find \nout more about how we can all work together to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195200,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avoid a climate disaster - like how we need \nhuge development in energy/grid storage in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "addition to renewable energy generation - \nyou can find out more in the link below."
    } ]
  } ]
}
